1
00:00:55,076 --> 00:01:00,328
Type any decision. Ripple reads it as you type.

2
00:01:00,328 --> 00:01:06,714
1,000 students, teachers and families react, starting from a real Pew poll.

3
00:01:06,714 --> 00:01:13,178
Ripple suggests a narrower version and re-runs the simulation.

4
00:01:13,178 --> 00:01:15,580
Phones only during class: from strong opposition to a real debate.

5
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:25,598
Watch opinions spread through the crowd over a year.

6
00:01:25,598 --> 00:01:29,034
Every group explains itself, and they debate in a town hall.

7
00:01:29,034 --> 00:01:37,896
Google Gemini turns the simulation into a full written report.

8
00:01:37,896 --> 00:01:43,539
Enter a ZIP code. The crowd becomes your district’s real Census population.

9
00:01:43,539 --> 00:01:56,574
Then run what your town actually decides: parks, bond votes, rent, school start times.

10
00:01:56,574 --> 00:02:04,461
27 real bills in Congress, updated every week from Congress.gov.

11
00:02:04,461 --> 00:02:09,578
Who really decides, and a letter that cites only real polls.

12
00:02:09,578 --> 00:02:14,532
Then ask real people: a 30-second survey with a QR code.

13
00:02:14,532 --> 00:02:19,294
Real answers appear next to Ripple’s prediction, group by group.

14
00:02:19,294 --> 00:02:24,678
Teachers start a class. Students join with a code and a nickname.

15
00:02:24,678 --> 00:02:28,284
Their simulations and surveys roll up for the teacher.

16
00:02:28,284 --> 00:02:44,249
Ripple works in Spanish, including decisions typed in Spanish.

17
00:02:44,249 --> 00:02:50,589
Every number shows its source, and Ripple checks itself against 40 real polls.
